WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.492
This lesson gets easier as soon as the learner stops treating temperature and internal energy as interchangeable labels.

2
00:00:06.492 --> 00:00:12.984
Temperature tells an average-per-particle story, while internal energy belongs to the whole sample.

3
00:00:12.984 --> 00:00:19.477
That difference is what makes equal-temperature comparison questions interesting.

4
00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:25.969
The safest habit is to ask first whether the question is about one typical particle or about the total sample ledger.

5
00:00:25.969 --> 00:00:32.461
Keep this takeaway in view: The lesson opens cleanly once temperature is treated as an average and internal energy as a total microscopic store.

6
00:00:32.461 --> 00:00:38.953
Temperature means average dash.

7
00:00:38.953 --> 00:00:45.446
Temperature tracks the average kinetic-energy level per particle, not the total store of the sample.

8
00:00:45.446 --> 00:00:51.938
Internal energy means whole-sample store.

9
00:00:51.938 --> 00:00:58.430
Internal energy belongs to every particle in the sample together, so particle number matters.

10
00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:05.262
This diagram matters because it keeps the average story and the whole-sample story visible at the same time.

11
00:01:05.262 --> 00:01:12.093
One layer tells you what temperature is saying about a typical particle.

12
00:01:12.093 --> 00:01:18.925
Another reminds you that internal energy depends on the full population of particles in the sample.

13
00:01:18.925 --> 00:01:25.757
Keep this takeaway in view: A single chamber picture removes the biggest average-versus-total mistake before calculation starts.

14
00:01:25.757 --> 00:01:32.588
Average view: Temperature answers the per-particle question by tracking average kinetic energy.

15
00:01:32.588 --> 00:01:39.420
Whole-sample view: Internal energy adds up the microscopic store across the entire sample.

16
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:45.270
A running-hall analogy is useful because it separates average pace from total activity.

17
00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:51.120
If each runner in both halls has the same average pace, the two halls match in the average sense.

18
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.970
But the hall with more runners still has the larger total activity.

19
00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:02.820
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy makes the average-versus-total distinction feel natural before any calculation starts.

20
00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:08.670
Average pace means temperature hook.

21
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:14.520
The shared pace stands for equal average kinetic energy per particle.

22
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:20.370
Crowd size means particle count.

23
00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:26.220
More runners means a larger overall activity total even if the average pace is unchanged.

24
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:31.901
For an ideal gas, temperature is tied to average kinetic energy per particle.

25
00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:37.582
Internal energy belongs to the whole microscopic store of the sample.

26
00:02:37.582 --> 00:02:43.263
So the safest route is to decide first whether the question is about an average or a total before bringing in particle count.

27
00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:48.944
That one decision prevents most of the confusion in the first worked example.

28
00:02:48.944 --> 00:02:54.625
Keep this takeaway in view: The learner is safer once temperature is treated as an average rule and internal energy as a whole-sample rule.

29
00:02:54.625 --> 00:03:00.305
Do not merge the stories: Average kinetic energy and total internal energy answer different questions.

30
00:03:00.305 --> 00:03:05.986
Kelvin matters later: Keep temperature tied to particle motion rather than to everyday hotness language alone.

31
00:03:05.986 --> 00:03:11.667
temperature -> average kinetic energy per particle.

32
00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:17.348
internal energy -> total microscopic store of the sample.

33
00:03:17.348 --> 00:03:23.029
Compare the average story first.

34
00:03:23.029 --> 00:03:28.710
Then ask how many particles are sharing that average level.

35
00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:33.973
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

36
00:03:33.973 --> 00:03:39.237
First compare temperature and decide what it says about average kinetic energy per particle.

37
00:03:39.237 --> 00:03:44.500
Then compare particle count.

38
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:49.763
Only after those two steps should you make the total internal-energy claim.

39
00:03:49.763 --> 00:03:55.027
Keep this takeaway in view: Average first, particle count second, total conclusion third is the safest opening routine.

40
00:03:55.027 --> 00:04:00.290
Step 1 means compare temperature.

41
00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:05.553
Equal kelvin temperature means equal average kinetic energy per particle for ideal-gas comparisons.

42
00:04:05.553 --> 00:04:10.817
Step 2 means compare amount.

43
00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:16.080
Check whether the particle count stays the same or scales up or down.

44
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:21.054
Run four checks.

45
00:04:21.054 --> 00:04:26.028
Equal temperature means equal average kinetic energy per particle.

46
00:04:26.028 --> 00:04:31.002
Equal temperature does not automatically mean equal internal energy.

47
00:04:31.002 --> 00:04:35.976
Particle count must be inspected before a total-energy claim.

48
00:04:35.976 --> 00:04:40.950
And more particles at the same average level means a larger total store.

49
00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:45.924
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can defend why equal temperature does not automatically mean equal internal energy.

50
00:04:45.924 --> 00:04:50.898
Average claim means same T -> same average.

51
00:04:50.898 --> 00:04:55.872
In the ideal-gas picture, temperature controls the average per-particle kinetic energy.

52
00:04:55.872 --> 00:05:00.846
Total claim means same T does not lock U.

53
00:05:00.846 --> 00:05:05.820
The total microscopic store can still change when the amount of gas changes.

54
00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:12.860
Start by using temperature to compare the average kinetic energy per particle in the two containers.

55
00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:19.900
Only after that should you inspect how many particles each container holds.

56
00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:26.940
Then use those two facts together to make the total internal-energy statement.

57
00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:33.980
Keep this takeaway in view: The first A6 worked example improves immediately once the learner separates average particle energy from whole-sample internal energy.

58
00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:41.020
Do not rush the total: The total internal-energy claim should be the final step, not the first instinct.

59
00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:48.060
Keep the wording precise: Say average particle kinetic energy when you mean temperature, and total microscopic store when you mean internal energy.

60
00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:55.100
temperature first -> compare average particle energy.

61
00:05:55.100 --> 00:06:02.140
particle count second -> compare the size of the sample.

62
00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:09.180
A good answer never jumps to the total before the average story is secure.
